"Jakob Creutzig" <creutzig@mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de> wrote:

> I think that, for the sake of fairness, the
> monster difficulty of a corpse should be
> determined in the moment of its decease (an Ogre
> king could eg drink a potion of gain level and get
> tougher, which should be reflected in the
> difficulty), and this would mean that a heavily
> level-drained monster leaves a corpse with little
> sacrifice value.  But is this the case?  

Well, no. Sacrificing a monster is a measure of
devotion to ones god(dess), there is little evidence
that the deity gains nourishment from the corpse,
more that the deity is nourished by the act. If a
player kills a fierce monster in part by draining
its levels, that is just one more of a large number
of ways (including: let Fido do it) to defeat a
monster. The corpse value to your deity should not
depend on _how_ you defeated the monster, but on
_whether_ you defeated the monster (and made the
harder (sometimes) choice to sacrifice it rather
than eat it)).

And after all, at the time you defeat the monster,
it has almost no remaining hit points, very likely,
and is bashed, bruised, cut, and maybe burned or
frozen, too. None of those should inveigh against
the value to your deity of the devotion shown to
kill and sacrifice such a monster.

IMO

